# github-pr

Own every GitHub PR review posted on the user's behalf. `review <n>` conducts one review where it is invoked, from the PR's diff with no checkout, triages findings with the user, and posts only after confirmation.

A bare `<n>` means `review <n>`, unless the authenticated user is the PR's author: then the user wants the author's side, which is the `drive-pr` skill, so say so and ask which they meant.

This skill may be invoked directly or by any agent. Both GitHub scripts below, and the schema of everything posted, are in [references/posting.md](references/posting.md); read it before reading a PR's threads or posting.

## Open with the summary

The PR's summary is how the user tells which PR is in front of them, so every review opens with it: before reading anything else about the PR, run the `summarize-github-pr` skill and send its output, every line as markdown, as your first message.

## Review the PR

Run `review <n>` inline, in the session that invokes it: review the PR as this section describes, then [triage with the user](#triage-with-the-user) and [post](#post).

A review reads the PR from git's object store rather than a checkout. Start with the head check in [Keep up with the head](#keep-up-with-the-head), which fetches the PR's head and base without touching the working tree and returns `head_sha`, `base_branch`, and `merge_base`, plus `stacked_on` and `stacked_under` for [Stacks](#stacks).

Read the PR title, author, body, and changed files, and run `scripts/feedback.py <n>` for the review conversation so far: my prior findings in the threads I started, and the open comments of other reviewers (see [Other reviewers' comments](#other-reviewers-comments)).

Invoke the shared `review` skill once per head with a self-contained brief; it may run in a subagent. The brief contains:

- the PR's stated intent and base branch
- the change scope, `git diff <merge_base> <head_sha>`, which is the diff GitHub shows
- that the working tree is not the PR's code, since it may be on any branch: read the PR's code with `git show <head_sha>:<path>` and search it with `git grep <pattern> <head_sha>`, and don't run its tests, which have no checkout to run in
- my unresolved prior findings and their thread comment IDs, to verify against the current code rather than raise again
- the other reviewers' open comments, as context, so it doesn't report what they already raised as if it were new
- for a stacked PR, the context [Stacks](#stacks) describes
- an explicit instruction not to report Nits

While the reviewer runs, tell the user in a line or two that it is running, what you asked it to focus on, and the stack the PR sits in, if any, such as `stacked on #41, with #45 and #46 built on it`; the summary that opened the session already describes the PR.

The shared reviewer returns Blockers and Follow-ups without editing, interaction, a verdict, or posting. Preserve its classifications unless discussion with the user supplies new technical evidence. Then take a stance on the other reviewers' comments yourself, as below, from its findings and your own reading of the code.

On a re-review, each prior finding the reviewer verifies as fixed is a thread to resolve: resolving is how the reviewer confirms a fix holds. A prior finding that still stands gets a reply on its thread instead of a new inline comment.

The review ends with these results, which triage works from:

- `head_sha`, `base_branch`, and `merge_base` as reviewed
- each finding, with its classification, title, and impact, the anchor it would post at (a path and a RIGHT-side line in the diff, or a short name for the summary body when nothing anchors; see [references/posting.md](references/posting.md)), and the comment it would post, prefixed `[Blocker]` or `[Follow-up]`
- each prior finding, with its thread's root comment ID, as fixed or still standing, and for one still standing, the reply it would post
- each other reviewer's comment it takes a stance on, with its thread's root comment ID or its review or comment ID, its author, the comment's point in a line, the stance with its severity, and the reply it would post
- what the reviewer verified, and anything it couldn't

### Stacks

A stacked PR is one piece of a larger change, and reviewing it alone misreads it: its diff starts from code another open PR introduces, and what it leaves out may be done by the PR above it. `fetch.py` reports both directions.

Read neighbouring PRs only through `gh`, never with `scripts/fetch.py`: its private ref for a PR is what that PR's own review, which may be running in this checkout, relies on to notice a push.

When `stacked_on` is set, this PR's base is that open PR's branch, so this PR's diff is against code still under review. Read the lower PR with `gh pr view <lower> --json title,body` and `gh pr diff <lower>`, which is GitHub's diff, and give them to the reviewer as context, with a brief that says the base is another open PR. Its code is already at `head_sha`, since this PR's head contains it; if `gh pr diff` fails on a very large PR, the title, body, and that code are enough. Findings on code the lower PR introduced belong to that PR's review, not this one.

When `stacked_under` is not empty, other open PRs build on this one. Give the reviewer the titles and bodies of up to five of them (`gh pr view <m> --json title,body`), the lowest numbers first, and read one's diff (`gh pr diff <m>`) only when it bears on a finding, so a Follow-up about something missing names the upper PR that already does it instead of asking for it. A PR from a long-lived branch can have dozens built on it; past five, tell the user how many were left unread when you say the reviewer is running.

### Other reviewers' comments

Other reviewers, people and bots alike, may have raised concerns already. Weighing in on them tells the author which ones matter and how much, so the review answers them as well as adding its own. From `feedback.py`, take:

- each unresolved thread started by someone other than me and the PR's author, in which I haven't replied (`replied_by_me` is false)
- each review summary body and conversation comment by another reviewer that is `after_my_latest`, leaving out status and tooling comments that raise no concern about the code, such as a CI or deploy-preview bot's

Judge each one against the code and take one of three stances. The shared `review` skill doesn't do this; its report covers only problems the change introduces, so the stances are yours, informed by what it found:

- **Agree**: the concern holds. The reply gives it my severity, `[Blocker]` or `[Follow-up]`, and adds whatever the review found that strengthens it. When my severity is higher than the comment's own framing, such as a bot's suggestion that would break something, the reply says so: that is how a comment is escalated to a Blocker.
- **Dispute**: the concern doesn't hold. The reply says why, with evidence from the code, plainly and without arguing.
- **No stance**: a Nit, a matter of taste, a point already settled in its thread, or one outside the change's scope. Nothing is posted.

A finding of the review's own that another reviewer already raised becomes an Agree on their comment rather than a new comment of mine.

In triage, each Agree or Dispute is an item like a finding. An included Agree counts toward the GitHub event at its severity, so agreeing that another reviewer's comment is a Blocker requests changes.

### Keep up with the head

The author may push while the review is open, and a review formed against older code can't post. So check the head when the review starts, before triage's first question, and before the final confirmation:

```bash
scripts/fetch.py <n>
```

Run it from any checkout of the repository; under a sandbox that proxies network access, it reaches `api.github.com` and `github.com`. It fetches the PR's current head to a private ref and its base branch to `origin/<base>`, and changes nothing in the working tree. It returns `moved: true`, with `previous_head` and `rewritten`, when the head differs from the last fetch.

At the start, review whatever head it returns. When it moves later, tell the user in a line that the head moved and what the new commits do, then review again before going on: the reviewer gets the same brief plus the commits since `previous_head` (`previous_head..head_sha`, or a diff between the two heads when `rewritten` says it was a force-push) and this pass's results so far with the user's rulings. Invoke the shared `review` skill again. Triage only what is new or materially changed. An item the user already ruled on that still stands keeps its ruling, and one the new commits fix drops out; name the dropped ones in triage's count line, such as `1 Follow-up; the hero height one is fixed by 066422b`.

## Triage with the user

Open triage with a one-line count of the items, such as `2 Blockers, 3 Follow-ups, and 2 replies to Copilot`, then ask about them. The items are the findings, the replies on my prior findings that still stand, and the Agree and Dispute replies to other reviewers.

Every item gets its own question and its own ruling, but the questions go out together, Blockers first. Asking one item per prompt ("Follow-up 1 of 5", wait, "Follow-up 2 of 5", …) costs the user a round trip per finding and gains nothing, because findings rarely depend on each other. Ask only whether each item should be included; do not ask for an overall verdict yet.

- With a structured question UI, use as few calls as it allows: AskUserQuestion takes four questions per call, so five items take two calls. Offer `Include` and `Exclude`; the UI's own free-text option opens discussion or lets the user rewrite or reclassify the item.
- Without one, number the items and ask `Include?` after each, so a reply like `1y 2n 3y` settles the batch and a fuller answer on any item opens discussion of it.

Hold an item back for a later round only when its question depends on another item's ruling: two findings with one root cause, where excluding or rewriting one changes what the other should say, or a reply whose stance turns on how another item is settled. Ask the item it depends on first, then the dependent one, worded for that ruling. When findings are that entangled, asking them as one item is often simpler.

Keep each question concise: what the item is and its concrete consequence. For a reply to another reviewer, name whose comment it answers and the stance, such as `Agree with Copilot, as a Blocker: the retry loop never exits`. Do not put the full technical body or file coordinates in the question unless the user asks to discuss it. Preserve each ruling as you proceed; when the user opens discussion on one item of a batch, settle it and keep the rulings the rest of the batch already gave.

Derive the GitHub event from the included items: any Blocker, whether my own finding or an Agree with another reviewer, means `REQUEST_CHANGES`; otherwise use `APPROVE`. A Follow-up never changes the event by itself. If nothing remains included, the review is an approval. When the review found nothing to triage, go straight to the confirmation.

After triage, show everything that will post and explicitly ask for confirmation before posting: the event, the included findings, the replies, and the threads to be resolved. Open the confirmation with the summary's table (number and title, author and URL, ready time and stack) copied from the PR's summary, running `summarize-github-pr` for it if this session hasn't, so the user can see which PR it is posting to. If the user declines, revise or stop as directed. Never infer posting approval from the earlier per-item rulings.

## Post

Render the confirmed content into a batch, with this exact header, so every posted message is attributed:

```markdown
{{ includeTemplate ".chezmoitemplates/posting-attribution.md" . | trim }}
```

The batch has one inline comment per included finding at its anchor, a finding with no anchor in the body under its name, `thread_replies` for the replies on my still-standing prior findings and on other reviewers' threads, and `resolve` for the verified fixes. Replies to other reviewers' summary bodies and conversation comments, which have no thread, go in the review's body, naming whose point each answers. When the event rests on an Agree at Blocker, the body names that point too, such as `Requesting changes for Copilot's point on the retry loop (see its thread)`, so the review says why even before its replies land.

Set `head_sha` to the head the content was formed against. Run `scripts/post.py --dry-run batch.json` and resolve every problem it reports. If the PR's head has moved, do not post content formed against the stale code: go back through [Keep up with the head](#keep-up-with-the-head) and confirm again. Only then run `scripts/post.py batch.json`. If a step fails after part of the batch landed, retry only with the remainder batch the poster prints, never the original. Do not fall back to hand-built `gh` posting if the script fails. Posting is the only external mutation this skill performs, and only after the confirmation above.

## Boundaries

- Do not edit the PR's source.
- Do not request or report Nits.
- Do not run more than one reviewer for the same PR in a session.
- Resolve only threads I started.
